HLS native diagnostics and producer lifetime
===========================================

FFmpeg stderr is continuously drained through a pipe. At most the first 64 KiB
is retained in the existing private 0600 per-session diagnostic file. Remaining
bytes are discarded without copying native text to application logs. A write
failure disables further retention but continues draining, so diagnostic disk
failure does not make the child block on a full pipe. The owned capture task
closes the file at EOF/cancellation and is awaited during session cleanup.
The prefix limit deliberately does not retain later messages or rotate logs.

A running producer can still be shared for an identical request. A dead live
producer or failed recorded producer is removed and replaced on the next start.
A successfully exited recorded producer can be reused only while its playlist
and at least one correctly named media segment remain. This avoids restarting
valid completed recordings while refusing dead sessions with no retained media.
This file-presence check does not inspect every media byte or guarantee decoding.

Stop retains terminate/kill deadlines, awaits the diagnostic task and removes
private session files. Worker-crash child binding remains the separate Linux
launcher introduced earlier; it does not itself remove crash-left files.

Tests exercise actual local children, bounded output, diagnostic-write failure,
stop/reap and completed-versus-failed reuse. Packaged qualification uses local
synthetic FFmpeg inputs; these checks do not claim vendor RTSP acceptance.
Multiworker/multihost session coordination, individual viewer leases, aggregate
session/resource quotas and abandoned directory cleanup remain separate work.
